PHP code:
$a = [1, 2];
$b = $a;
$b[] = 3;
echo count($a);

In PHP, arrays are passed by value (copy on write). So $b = $a creates a copy of $a. Then $b[] = 3 modifies $b, not $a. So $a remains [1, 2], and count($a) is 2.

We have: function inc(&$x) { $x++; } $n = 5; inc($n); echo $n;

The function takes $x by reference, increments it. $n is 5, passed by reference, so $n becomes 6. echo $n outputs 6.

